{% extends "guide-caution.njk" %}

{% block section_0 %}
<p>
  Spanish is the second most spoken language in the world by native speakers, with over 500 million people across 20+ countries — from Spain and Mexico to Colombia, Argentina, and the large US Hispanic population.<br>
  The opportunity is enormous, but a common mistake is treating "Spanish" as a single monolithic market. The Spanish spoken in Madrid, Mexico City, and Buenos Aires differs in vocabulary, spelling conventions, formality levels, and cultural references — and users notice immediately when they're being addressed with the wrong variant.<br>
  This guide covers what you need to know about technical setup, SEO, design, and conversion for Spanish-language websites that actually work across different markets.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_1_1 %}
<p>
  Spanish varies significantly by region, and the variant you use sends an immediate signal about which audience you're addressing. The key distinctions:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
  <li><strong>Spain Spanish (es-ES):</strong> Uses <em>vosotros</em> (plural "you"), distinct pronunciation (the "theta" sound for c/z), and European spelling conventions. Formal tone is standard in business communications.</li>
  <li><strong>Latin American Spanish (es-419 or country-specific):</strong> Uses <em>ustedes</em> instead of <em>vosotros</em>, and is generally perceived as warmer and less formal. Covers the largest share of the global Spanish-speaking population.</li>
  <li><strong>Argentina / Uruguay:</strong> Uses <em>vos</em> instead of <em>tú</em>, with distinct conjugations. Argentinan Spanish has strong Italian-language influences in rhythm and vocabulary.</li>
  <li><strong>US Hispanic (es-US):</strong> A large and growing digital audience. Often bilingual — English/Spanish code-switching is common — and tends toward informal, conversational Spanish.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  For a broad Latin American audience, neutral Latin American Spanish (avoiding strong regionalism) is the safest default. Always set <code>&lt;html lang="es"&gt;</code> or a country-specific variant like <code>lang="es-MX"</code>.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_1_2 %}
<p>
  Spanish-speaking markets are largely open internet environments — Google, Facebook, Instagram, and WhatsApp work freely in all major Spanish-speaking countries. There are no Great Firewall-style restrictions.
</p>
<p>
  The main technical considerations are around <strong>mobile performance</strong> — particularly in Latin America, where mobile internet on lower-cost Android devices is the primary access method for a large portion of the population.
</p>
<div style="background:#fff4f4; padding:16px; border-radius:8px; border:1px solid #f9a8a8; margin-bottom:24px;">
  <p style="font-weight:bold; color:#c53030; margin-bottom:8px;">❌ Common performance pitfalls</p>
  <ul style="list-style:none; padding:0; margin:0;">
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">· Large uncompressed images that stall loading on 3G/4G connections</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:8px;">· Heavy JavaScript frameworks that perform poorly on mid-range Android devices</li>
    <li>· Failing Core Web Vitals — Google ranks Spanish-language pages by the same standards globally</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>
  Aim for a page weight under 1MB and test on a simulated mid-range mobile connection. Google's PageSpeed Insights is your benchmark.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_1_3 %}
<p>
  Legal requirements vary by country, but for the two largest markets:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
  <li><strong>Spain (EU member — GDPR applies):</strong> All GDPR obligations apply — cookie consent banner, privacy policy, data subject rights. Spain's national authority (AEPD) actively enforces compliance. Spanish-language privacy policies must be provided.</li>
  <li><strong>Mexico (Ley Federal de Protección de Datos Personales):</strong> Mexico's federal data protection law requires a privacy notice (<em>aviso de privacidad</em>) informing users what data is collected and how it's used. Less prescriptive than GDPR but still legally required.</li>
  <li><strong>E-commerce across Latin America:</strong> Consumer protection laws vary by country. If selling to consumers (B2C), familiarize yourself with local return policies, tax collection requirements (e.g. Mexico's VAT on digital services for foreign providers), and invoicing obligations.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  For businesses only targeting Spain, GDPR compliance is the clearest legal framework to follow. For Latin America, aim at minimum for Mexico's requirements as a common baseline.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_2_1 %}
<p>
  Spanish-speaking audiences respond well to warm, relationship-oriented communication. The dominant B2B and B2C content patterns differ meaningfully from Anglo-American norms:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Relationship before transaction:</strong> In Latin American business culture, trust is built before deals are made. Content that emphasizes your team, company story, and track record resonates more than pure benefit-first copy. "Who are you?" matters as much as "What do you offer?"</li>
  <li><strong>Testimonials and social proof:</strong> Reviews and case studies are highly influential — particularly from recognizable local brands or personalities.</li>
  <li><strong>WhatsApp as a contact channel:</strong> WhatsApp is the dominant messaging app across Latin America and Spain. Displaying a WhatsApp contact button is often more effective than a form or email, especially for SMB audiences.</li>
  <li><strong>Keyword research in Spanish:</strong> Spanish has significant regional vocabulary variation. "Carro" (car) is used in Mexico, "coche" in Spain, "auto" in Argentina. Use Google Keyword Planner filtered by specific countries, not just "Spanish."</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_2_2 %}
<p>
  <strong>Google dominates search in all major Spanish-speaking markets</strong> — both Spain and Latin America. Standard Google SEO best practices apply, with some Spanish-specific considerations:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>hreflang tags are essential:</strong> If you run multiple Spanish variants (e.g. es-ES and es-MX), use <code>hreflang</code> tags to tell Google which page to serve for each region. Without them, Google may serve the wrong variant or treat your pages as duplicate content.</li>
  <li><strong>Local keyword research per market:</strong> Spanish vocabulary varies enough by country that keyword research must be done per target market, not generically for "Spanish."</li>
  <li><strong>E-E-A-T signals:</strong> Same as English — author credentials, citations, and update dates improve trustworthiness in Google's eyes.</li>
  <li><strong>Voice search:</strong> Spanish speakers have high rates of voice search use on mobile. Optimize for conversational, question-based queries in your target market's dialect.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_2_3 %}
<p>
  For Spanish-speaking markets, the optimal server strategy depends on your primary geographic target:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
  <li><strong>Spain / Europe:</strong> EU-region servers (Ireland, Frankfurt, Madrid) — same recommendations as any European market.</li>
  <li><strong>Mexico and North/Central America:</strong> US East or US West coast servers, or a CDN with Latin American PoPs (Cloudflare and AWS CloudFront both have presence in São Paulo, Bogotá, and Mexico City).</li>
  <li><strong>South America:</strong> AWS São Paulo or similar Latin American region for minimum latency to Argentina, Chile, Colombia, and Brazil (note: Brazil uses Portuguese, not Spanish).</li>
</ul>
<p>
  For domain choice: <code>.com</code> is universally trusted across all Spanish-speaking markets. Country-specific TLDs like <code>.mx</code>, <code>.es</code>, or <code>.co</code> can boost local trust and SEO for a specific country, but require local entity registration.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_3_1 %}
<p>
  Spanish-speaking cultures tend to respond well to warmer, more saturated color palettes compared to Northern European or Anglo-American design conventions:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0;">
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🔴 <strong>Red and orange:</strong> Energy, passion, warmth — particularly resonant across Latin American markets. Red is widely used for CTAs and promotional messaging.</li>
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🟡 <strong>Yellow and warm gold:</strong> Optimism, celebration, and festivity. Common in food, retail, and consumer brands targeting Latin American audiences.</li>
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🟢 <strong>Green:</strong> Nature, health, and freshness. Popular in organic, agricultural, and wellness categories. In Mexico, the national flag's green also carries patriotic associations.</li>
  <li style="margin-bottom: 12px;">🔵 <strong>Blue:</strong> Trusted and professional across the region — particularly for B2B, finance, and government-adjacent services. Works equally well in Spain and Latin America.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Spain leans slightly more toward minimalist European design conventions; Latin American markets generally respond better to bolder, more visually energetic layouts.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_3_2 %}
<p>
  Spanish uses the Latin alphabet with a few additional characters: ñ, á, é, í, ó, ú, ü, and ¿¡ for question and exclamation marks. Most standard Latin web fonts handle these correctly, but always verify rendering for special characters.
</p>
<div style="background:#f1f5f9; padding:12px; border-radius:4px; font-family:monospace; margin-bottom:16px;">
  font-family: "Inter", "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
</div>
<p>
  Standard Latin fonts (Inter, Roboto, Source Sans, Open Sans) all support Spanish characters natively. The key is ensuring your font files include the full Latin Extended character set — some subset fonts strip out accented characters.
</p>
<p>
  Body text at 16px, line-height 1.6–1.8. Spanish sentences tend to be longer than English equivalents (more articles, gendered adjectives, longer verbs), so line length and column width deserve more attention — narrow columns create uncomfortable line breaks.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_3_3 %}
<p>
  Design preferences vary somewhat between Spain and Latin America, but some patterns hold broadly across Spanish-speaking markets:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Human photography performs well:</strong> Images featuring real people — particularly those who look like the target audience — outperform abstract or product-only visuals. Authenticity matters.</li>
  <li><strong>Moderate information density:</strong> More visual than Northern European markets, but less dense than East Asian markets. Clear visual hierarchy with generous section spacing works well.</li>
  <li><strong>Mobile-first layout:</strong> Especially critical in Latin America, where many users browse exclusively on smartphone. Single-column layouts with large tap targets are the baseline requirement.</li>
  <li><strong>Video content:</strong> Video consumption is extremely high across Spanish-speaking markets. Embedding a short explainer or product video on your landing page significantly increases engagement.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_4_1 %}
<p>
  Contact preferences in Spanish-speaking markets lean toward direct, conversational communication — particularly for SMBs and consumer audiences:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>WhatsApp Business:</strong> Displaying a WhatsApp chat button is the single most effective CTA for Latin American audiences. It's the primary way people communicate with businesses in the region. WhatsApp Business API enables automated responses and catalog features.</li>
  <li><strong>Phone contact:</strong> A local phone number builds significant trust, especially in markets where consumers are cautious about foreign businesses. Consider a virtual number through services like Twilio for your target country.</li>
  <li><strong>Contact forms:</strong> Keep them short. Name, email, and message is the right default — lengthy forms have very high abandonment rates, especially on mobile.</li>
  <li><strong>Social media:</strong> Instagram is dominant for B2C across Latin America; LinkedIn for B2B. Facebook remains strong particularly in older demographics and smaller cities. Displaying active social profiles reinforces legitimacy.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

{% block section_4_2 %}
<p>
  Mobile is the primary internet access device for a large share of Spanish-speaking users, particularly in Latin America. In countries like Mexico, Colombia, and Peru, <strong>smartphone penetration exceeds desktop internet usage</strong> significantly.
</p>
<p>
  Key considerations:
</p>
<ul style="list-style: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
  <li><strong>Test on mid-range Android devices:</strong> Samsung Galaxy A-series and similar mid-range Android phones represent a large portion of the device market in Latin America. Performance on these devices is often worse than on iPhones or flagship Android devices.</li>
  <li><strong>Lightweight pages:</strong> Data costs are proportionally higher in many Latin American markets — users are sensitive to pages that consume significant mobile data.</li>
  <li><strong>WhatsApp click-to-chat links:</strong> <code>https://wa.me/[number]</code> links open WhatsApp directly from the browser. Make this your primary mobile CTA for Latin American audiences.</li>
  <li><strong>Tap target sizing:</strong> Minimum 44×44px for all interactive elements.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}

